# Files whose identity IS their exact bytes, held to LF in the working tree on every
# platform. Git stores them LF and, with core.autocrlf on (the Windows default), hands
# the checkout CRLF - so the bytes a Windows clone reads are not the bytes the repo
# holds, and anything that hashes or executes them reads a different file than CI does.
#
# The vendored delivery helpers are pinned by SHA-256 (devops/vendor/PINNED.json): the
# pin is byte-exact by design, which is what makes a hand-edit detectable at all
# (INT-FC-R112). Translated on checkout, every hash misses and the guard reports the
# drift it exists to detect on a tree that has none - a false alarm whose own advice
# (re-pin) would record the translated bytes and break the pin for everyone else.
#
# Shell scripts carry a shebang: a CRLF line ending makes the kernel look for an
# interpreter named `bash\r`, so a script checked out on Windows fails to run both
# locally and, if it reaches an image build from that clone, inside the container.
*.sh                    text eol=lf
devops/vendor/*         text eol=lf
orchestrator/vendor/**  text eol=lf
# Redundant against the line above, and stated anyway: the pin guard asserts LF per
# DESTINATION, so a new vendored source that forgot this is caught rather than silently
# relying on an ancestor pattern somebody may narrow later.
orchestrator/vendor/promote/**  text eol=lf

devops/vendor/*.tar.gz -text
